.TH XMRENDITION 3 2026-09-08 "Motif 2.6.0"
.SH NAME
XmRendition - Motif Font Abstraction

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <Xm/Xm.h>
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBXmRendition\fP is a pseudo-widget used as an abstraction over the
various font types available on the system. This data structure is
intentionally opaque, and contains information about a particular
font, and certain stylistic parameters, which are exposed though the
resources given below. \fBXmRendition\fP's resources can be accessed
through the \fBXmRenditionGetValues\fP and \fBXmRenditionSetValues\fP
functions, patterned after \fBXtGetValues\fP and \fBXtSetValues\fP. As
it is not a true \fBWidget\fP, calling the Xt functions on it will
certainly fail.

Renditions inform the compound string (\fBXmString\fP) drawing code about
how a particular string, or part of a string, should be drawn. This
association is based on matching the rendition's tag against that of the
compound string within a \fBXmRenderTable\fP. Though stylistic
parameters such as color and line type are available on the rendition,
primarily for establishing defaults, full advantage should be taken of
the \fBXmRenditionStyle\fP type to convey programmatically-defined
instances of those properties for drawing rather than modifying those
properties of the rendition.

.SH FONT LOADING
For \fBXmRendition\fP to load a font, it must have a load model, type,
and pattern. The pattern (\fIXmNfontName\fP) is the textual string used
to load the font. This will be a XLFD string for X fonts and fontsets,
or a Fontconfig pattern string for Xft fonts.

Modifying font specification resources (\fIXmNfontName\fP,
\fIXmNfontType\fP, \fIXmNfont\fP, \fIXmNxftFont\fP, \fIXmNloadModel\fP)
of a rendition will cause the underlying font to be unloaded and reloaded,
save for externally loaded fonts (see below). Setting \fIXmNfont\fP or
\fIXmNxftFont\fP will force the loading model to \fBXmUNSPECIFIED_LOAD_MODEL\fP.

A rendition can be checked to see if the font was loaded successfully
with \fBXmRenditionIsLoaded\fP. Loading failures will generally emit a
warning message to standard output.

.SS IMMEDIATE LOADING
By specifying the load model as \fBXmLOAD_IMMEDIATE\fP, the font will be
loaded when the \fBXmRendition\fP object is created.

.SS DEFERRED LOADING
A rendition may have the load model \fBXmLOAD_DEFERRED\fP, indicating that
the underlying font will be materialized the first time the rendition is
used to render a compound string or a part thereof; or the first time its
resource properties are queried.

.SS ON-DEMAND LOADING
The \fBXmLOAD_LAZY\fP model indicates that the user will explicitly
manage the loading of the font using the \fBXmRenditionLoad\fP and
\fBXmRenditionUnload\fP functions.

.SS EXTERNAL LOADING
\fBXmUNSPECIFIED_LOAD_MODEL\fP is used for cases where the user wants
to handle the font loading themselves. This is acheived by specifying
the \fIXmNfontType\fP and the corresponding \fIXmNfont\fP or \fIXmNxftFont\fP
property. Motif will not take ownership of such fonts, and will expect
the user to manage their lifecycle. Care must be taken to ensure that
the font type agrees with the provided font object.

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS LOADING A FONT
The simplest way to load a font is by passing a perpared set of arguments
to \fBXmRenditionCreate\fP. For example, to load the X font "fixed":
.PP
.EX
Arg arg[3];
XmRendition r;

XtSetArg(arg[0], XmNloadModel, XmLOAD_IMMEDIATE);
XtSetArg(arg[1], XmNfontType, XmFONT_IS_FONT);
XtSetArg(arg[2], XmNfontName, "fixed");
r = XmRenditionCreate(NULL, XmSTRING_DEFAULT_CHARSET, arg, 3);
if (r && XmRenditionIsLoaded(r)) {
	/* ... */
	XmRenditionFree(r);
}
.EE

.SH LOADING A XFT FONT
For Xft fonts, the metadata properties may be specified in the arguments
given to \fBXmRenditionCreate\fP. These are added to the Fontconfig pattern
that will be matched to load the font. When the font is loaded, the
font-supplied values will replace them. A 12-point sans-serif font could
thus be loaded:
.PP
.EX
XtSetArg(arg[0], XmNloadModel, XmLOAD_IMMEDIATE);
XtSetArg(arg[1], XmNfontType, XmFONT_IS_XFT);
XtSetArg(arg[2], XmNfontName, "sans-serif");
XtSetArg(arg[3], XmNfontSize, 12);
r = XmRenditionCreate(NULL, XmSTRING_DEFAULT_CHARSET, arg, 4);
.EE

